course: everyday
lesson: 375
title: What fights cost at school
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Jake was suspended for three days after a fight in the dinner hall. At first he thought it was a holiday. It wasn't: he spent the days doing schoolwork at his gran's, missed the planning for the class trip, and came back behind in maths. Then his new secondary school asked about the suspension, because it was on his record. Luckily his primary head also wrote that Jake had improved a lot. Records remember, so it is worth giving them good news.
explain: Fights cost other people too. When Jake started a fight in the line, the whole class lost golden time, and nobody wanted to sit with him at lunch. Maddie, the best striker, got a detention for fighting and missed the girls' football tournament; she watched from the sidelines as the team lost 2–1.
explain: After the nose punch, Maddie and Connor had to sit with Mr Pryce and say what THEY did, not what the other did. It was awkward. In the end Maddie said sorry for shoving, Connor said sorry for punching, and he admitted «hit like a boy» was a stupid thing to say. Kieran and Jake had a «restorative meeting» too, and found out their whole fight came from a rumour neither of them had started. They shook hands.
explain: Jake was banned from after-school football for a term and not allowed in the school play, though secretly he wanted to be the pirate. Sam's mum asked the school to keep Sam away from Jake, and that hurt; Jake quietly tried to show Sam he wasn't dangerous. But doors that close can open again: Jake's uncle took him to a boxing club where the rule was no fighting outside the ring, ever, and the next year, after a good term, Jake played the pirate and his whole family came.
fact: A suspension (also called an exclusion) means a pupil must stay away from school for a set number of days.
fact: A suspension is not a holiday; the pupil still has to do schoolwork and falls behind.
fact: Serious incidents like suspensions are written on a pupil's school record, which a new school can see.
fact: A school record can also show that a pupil has improved.
fact: Punishments for fighting often take away the things a child loves most, like a tournament or a play.
fact: When a fight disrupts a class, the whole group may lose time or treats.
fact: A restorative meeting is a talk where people who fell out say what happened and how to put it right.
fact: Owning your own part, instead of blaming the other person, is the heart of making peace.
fact: After a fight, other families may want to keep their children away; trust has to be earned back.
fact: Bans and lost chances can end when a pupil's behaviour changes.
exercise: Jake was suspended for three days. What did he think at first? | That it was a holiday from school | That he had been expelled for ever | That he had won a prize for his work | That his teacher was off sick all week
exercise: What did the three days really bring? | Schoolwork at his gran's and falling behind in maths | Three whole days of games and TV on the sofa at his gran's | A trip to the seaside | Nothing at all
exercise: Why did Jake's new secondary school ask about the suspension? | It was on his school record | Jake had told them all about it | Mr Pryce rang them up to complain | Every new pupil is asked that
exercise: What helped Jake with his new school? | His head wrote that he had improved a lot | He pretended it never happened | His mum told the new school that the old one was wrong about him | He moved to a different town
exercise: Why did Maddie miss the girls' football tournament? | She had a detention for fighting | She had hurt her ankle | The coach did not like her and picked someone else | She forgot the date
exercise: How did Maddie feel watching from the sidelines? | Gutted, because she was the best striker | Glad to have a rest | Bored and not bothered | Pleased, because she never really liked the team anyway
exercise: What happened to the class after Jake started a fight in the line? | The whole class lost golden time | Only Jake lost his lunch break that day | The whole class got extra play time | Nothing, it was only Jake's problem
exercise: Why did nobody want to sit with Jake at lunch that day? | The class was cross about losing golden time | He had a cold | He smelled of peas | He had asked to sit on his own at the back of the class
exercise: What is a restorative meeting? | A talk where both sides say what happened and how to put it right | A meeting to decide which one of them gets the worst punishment | A boxing match in the hall with a referee | A meeting with the police
exercise: What did Kieran and Jake find out in their meeting? | Their fight came from a rumour neither had started | Kieran had stolen Jake's new bike from outside the shop | Jake's mum knew Kieran's mum | They were cousins
exercise: Mr Pryce asked Maddie and Connor to say what THEY did, not what the other did. Why? | Each should own their own part | So the other one gets more blame | Because he already knew everything | To make the meeting a lot shorter
exercise: What did Connor admit at the end? | Saying «hit like a boy» was a stupid thing to say | Maddie had punched him first, so he only hit back | He did nothing wrong at all | He wanted to fight again
exercise: Jake was banned from after-school football for a term. What did his uncle do? | Took him to a boxing club where fighting outside the ring meant you're out | Told him to go and play football out in the street instead | Said bans don't matter | Taught him to fight in the park
exercise: Sam's mum asked school to keep Sam away from Jake. How did Jake take it? | It hurt, and he quietly tried to show Sam he wasn't dangerous | He was pleased to be left alone | He went round to Sam's house to shout at Sam's mum on the doorstep | He started a fight with Sam
exercise: Jake was not allowed in the school play. What happened the next year? | After a good term, he was the pirate and his family came | He was banned again for ever | He refused to be in it because plays are boring | He played the back end of the horse, which was the worst part
exercise: Which is a kind of courage shown in this lesson? | Saying sorry for your own part in a meeting | Thinking a suspension is a holiday | Blaming the other person for everything that went wrong | Hiding a detention from your team
test: «suspended 3 days?? lucky, free holiday». Is it? | No, you do the work at home, fall behind and miss things | Yes, three days of fun | Yes, and the school pays your family for the days off | Only if it is in summer
test: «nobody'll know about it at big school, it's just primary». What's true? | It goes on your record and they can see it | Records are thrown away every summer | Only your mum knows | Big school never asks
test: «why are WE losing golden time? it was Jake, not us!» What's the honest answer? | When a fight disrupts the class, the whole group pays | The whole class all started the fight together | Golden time was cancelled anyway | The teacher just didn't like any of them very much this term
test: Teacher in the meeting: «Maddie, tell me what YOU did — not Connor». What's the best start? | «I shoved him first» | «He punched me, that's what happened» | «I didn't do anything» | «Ask Connor, it's his fault»
test: «she started it so I don't have to say sorry». In a restorative meeting what's wise? | Say sorry for your part, whatever she did | Wait for her to say sorry first before you say anything | Say sorry but not mean it | Leave the meeting
test: «the whole scrap was over what Kieran supposedly said about your mum». In the meeting it turns out he never said it. What does that show? | Talking it through can show how silly a fight was | Rumours are always true | Kieran is only lying about the rumour now to get out of trouble | You should fight the rumour-starter instead
test: «I'm missing the final cos of a detention for one stupid fight, it's so unfair». What's the real lesson? | One fight can cost you the thing you love most | Detentions should never be allowed to fall on match days at all | The coach was always against her | The team didn't need her anyway
test: «Sam's mum don't want him near me, I never even hit Sam!» What's true? | After a fight, trust has to be earned back | Sam's mum has no right to worry about who Sam plays with | Jake should go round and argue | Sam must have lied
test: Jake's banned from football club. Uncle says «boxing club, but fight outside the ring and you're out». Why might that help? | It gives his energy a place with strict rules | It teaches him how to win street fights properly | It means he can fight anywhere he likes now | Boxing has no rules
test: «once you're banned from the play, you're banned for life». Is it? | No, doors can open again after you change | Yes, for ever | Yes, for ever, unless your family pays the school some money | Only if the head forgets
test: «your mate got suspended, so you're a troublemaker too». What's fair? | Each person is judged by what they did | Friends must share every single punishment | You should get yourself suspended too, to keep him company | Stop being his friend at once and for good
exam: Identify the principal purpose of a restorative meeting convened between pupils following a fight. | To permit each party to state their own conduct and to agree how the harm may be repaired | To determine which of the two parties merits the more severe punishment | To permit the parties to resume the fight under supervision | To inform the police of the incident
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a suspension from school? | A period of compulsory absence during which work continues and the pupil falls behind | A period of leisure granted to the pupil in recognition of good conduct | A permanent removal from the school without record | An arrangement without consequence for the pupil's education
exam: It is given that a fight initiated by one pupil disrupted a lesson. Ascertain the consequence that may fall upon the class as a whole. | The forfeiture of a shared privilege, such as golden time | The award of additional free time | No consequence whatsoever | The suspension of every pupil who was present in the classroom at the time
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a pupil's school record. | It may record both serious incidents and subsequent improvement, and may be seen by a new school | It records solely academic marks | It is destroyed upon the pupil's transfer to a new school, so that every pupil may begin entirely afresh | It may be viewed only by the pupil
